# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# opt-trusted: every workload on this machine is first-party code.
#
# The distinction that matters is not "our code vs their code" in the moral
# sense -- it is whether two mutually distrusting things share the machine.
# If they do not, several expensive defences are protecting nobody from
# nobody.
#
# THE RULE THIS FRAGMENT FOLLOWS: relax things whose cost is structural (a
# translation on every DMA, a sandbox nobody enters), and NEVER compile out a
# speculation mitigation.  Mitigations stay in the image and are relaxed on
# the command line, because a trust assumption is a statement about today's
# deployment and the command line can be changed when it stops being true.
# A kernel with L1TF compiled out cannot be fixed by a reboot.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- The big one: IOMMU passthrough -----------------------------------------
# Identity-mapped DMA instead of a translated domain.  This removes the IOMMU
# page-table walk and the IOTLB miss from the DMA path of every high-rate
# device -- which is exactly the "hardware passthrough for trusted workloads"
# trade: the device can DMA anywhere in host memory, and that is acceptable
# only because the driver and the device are both first-party.
#
# NEVER set this on a machine with tenant-assigned devices.  On those, the
# strict domain (40-platform / layer-hypervisor) is the thing standing between
# a tenant's VF and everyone else's memory.
# CONFIG_IOMMU_DEFAULT_DMA_STRICT is not set
CONFIG_IOMMU_DEFAULT_PASSTHROUGH=y

# --- No sandboxes -----------------------------------------------------------
# layer-containers carries no KVM: tenant isolation on this platform is the VM
# boundary a layer down, and a first-party metal node has nothing to sandbox.
# (Kept as a statement; there is nothing to strip.)

# --- Cache partitioning -----------------------------------------------------
# RDT/CLOS matters on shared first-party metal too: a batch data-loader can
# evict a serving process's working set from L3 and no cgroup stops it.
CONFIG_X86_CPU_RESCTRL=y

# --- Free-time poisoning ----------------------------------------------------
# Alloc-time init stays on everywhere.  Free-time poisoning is a
# use-after-free exploitation mitigation, and the attacker it assumes is code
# running on this machine.
# CONFIG_INIT_ON_FREE_DEFAULT_ON is not set

# --- Deliberately NOT relaxed -----------------------------------------------
# Every MITIGATION_* stays compiled in (see the rule above).  So do seccomp,
# landlock, lockdown and the KSPP set: they are cheap, and "first-party" is a
# statement about intent, not about the absence of bugs.  A memory-safe
# service that gets a malicious input still wants the kernel underneath it to
# be hard to escalate through.
